# SYNOPSIS

```usage
vx9pserve --listen addr:port dir
vx9pserve --stdio dir
```

# DESCRIPTION

`vx9pserve` serves the host directory <dir> over 9P2000, for VectraOS to mount over TCP with `mount` (see rc(1)).
It is a file server built on `lib/vx-9p`'s server framework, the one VectraOS's own servers use, and runs on Linux.

: `--listen` <addr>`:`<port>
  Listen on the IPv4 address <addr>, port <port>, and serve each connection in a process of its own.
: `--stdio`
  Serve one connection, on standard input and output, until it ends.

Under QEMU's user networking, build(1)'s `qemu` and `test` give each connection to 10.0.2.100 port 5640 a `vx9pserve --stdio` of its own, through QEMU's `guestfwd`, so no host port is used.
`./build qemu` serves `out/share`; each test scenario serves a fresh copy of `tests/fixtures/share` in its run directory, which the scenario's `host=` records check after the run.
QEMU does not forward the gateway's own address, so to mount `tcp!10.0.2.2!5640`, the host as the guest sees it, run `vx9pserve --listen 127.0.0.1:5640` <dir> on the host.

Nothing outside <dir> can be reached.
The framework keeps every walk inside the attach root and refuses the names `.`, the empty name, and names holding `/`; the server opens each path one component at a time from a descriptor on <dir>, never following a symbolic link, so a link anywhere, even one put there after a walk, is refused.
Only directories and regular files are seen: links, devices, sockets and FIFOs are neither listed nor walked to.

Each file is numbered the first time it is walked to, and the number is its qid path for as long as the server runs; its qid version is its modification time in seconds.
Every file and directory is owned by `host`, user and group, and shows the host's permission bits.
The server keeps no descriptors but the root's: each read and write opens the file again.

What a client may do:

- `Tversion`, with an msize of at most 65536; no 9Px extension is granted.
- `Tattach`, with an empty aname only; `Tauth` is refused.
- `Twalk`, `Tstat`, `Tread` and `Twrite` as 9P2000 has them, and `Topen` with the host's permissions checked, truncating with `OTRUNC`.
- `Tcreate` of a file, or of a directory, with the permission bits asked for, less the host's umask; a file's are cut to 0666.
- `Tremove` of a file or an empty directory, but not of the root.

# EXAMPLES

A mount from the console, as the mount test does it, with the console's other output removed:

```rc
% mount tcp!10.0.2.100!5640 /n/host
% ls /n/host
hello.txt
% cat /n/host/hello.txt
hello from the host
% echo hi > /n/host/out.txt
% cat /n/host/out.txt
hi
% mount 9p://10.0.2.100:5640 /n/again
% ls /n/again
hello.txt  out.txt
```

